#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_roots

#% text
# `n_roots(p == q, x)` gives the numerical roots of a polynomial equation in x as `x == r1 or x == r2 or ...`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Roots are repeated by their multiplicity.

#% item
# A polynomial p alone is taken as `p == 0`.

#% item
# `precision_goal: d` with d above machine precision gives the roots with d digits (computed with that working precision).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
n_roots(1 + 2*x + 3*x^2 + 4*x^3 == 0, x)
#% output
#= x == -0.605829586188268 or x == -0.07208520690586599 - 0.6383267351483765im or x == -0.07208520690586599 + 0.6383267351483765im

#% cell
n_roots(x^3 - 5*x^2 + 6*x - 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= x == 0.19806226419516174 or x == 1.554958132087371 or x == 3.246979603717467

#% cell
n_roots((x - 1)*(x - 2)^2 == 0, x)
#% output
#= x == 1.0 or x == 2.0 or x == 2.0

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The roots with 30 digits:

#% cell
n_roots(x^3 - 5*x^2 + 6*x - 1 == 0, x, precision_goal: 30)
#% output
#= x == 0.19806226419516174752779536098510990 or x == 1.5549581320873711914221948710064105 or x == 3.2469796037174670610500097680084796

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `n_solve`, `roots`, `solve`
